{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"bare-coed-thrasher","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/reducer/_processors.py` measures **777 lines** against the hard 800-line module-size gate\n(`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), which CI enforces with no allowlist. The sibling story that\nprojects two new ticket state fields needs more than the 23 remaining lines.\n\n## Approach\n\nExtract the STATUS-processing cluster into a new `_processors_status.py`: `_fold_plan_review_phase`\n(`:32-38`), `_fold_claimed_session` (`:187-208`), `_fold_close_class` (`:211-225`) and\n`process_status` (`:228-328`).\n\nThe seam already exists and is closed: `process_status` is the **sole caller** of all three folds,\nat `:279,281,282` in the fork-winner branch and `:307,308,309` in the normal branch. The one function\nthat might have made the cluster leaky does not — `process_snapshot` sets `plan_review_phase` inline\nat `:705-710` rather than calling `_fold_plan_review_phase`.\n\nThe sibling `_processors_identity.py` (179 lines) is the exact precedent, and its own header states\nthe contract this follows: it imports nothing from `_processors`, so the split stays one-way, and\n`_processors` re-exports every symbol for back-compat. The re-export block at `_processors.py:15-22`\nis the pattern to mirror.\n\nThe alternative cut — the CREATE/genesis cluster (`process_create` `:40-152` plus\n`_project_legacy_creation_channel` `:155-184`, 143 lines) — is also valid but strictly weaker:\n`_project_legacy_creation_channel` has a second caller in `process_snapshot` (`:777`), so the\nextracted symbol would have to be imported back. The STATUS cluster has no such back-edge.\n\nThis is a behaviour-preserving move: no logic is rewritten.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/reducer/_processors_status.py` (new) — the moved cluster, ~171 lines\n- `src/rebar/reducer/_processors.py` — drops to ~632 lines, plus a re-export line\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new behaviour, so the oracle is the existing suite passing unchanged. This extraction is the\ncheapest of the set — the new module's only import is `logging`, and it touches no other module-level\nname — so the risk concentrates entirely on the re-export:\n\n- Re-export assertion: `from rebar.reducer._processors import process_status` still resolves. Four\n  consumers depend on it — the reducer dispatch table at `src/rebar/reducer/_replay.py:32`, and three\n  test modules (`tests/unit/test_attestations_reducer.py:17`,\n  `tests/unit/test_harness_provenance.py:20`, `tests/unit/test_plan_review_phase_reducer.py:10`) —\n  and all four must stay green with **zero** test edits.\n- Replay equivalence: replaying a fixture ticket through the reducer yields byte-identical state\n  before and after the move. This is the assertion that actually proves behaviour preservation for a\n  reducer, since a status fold silently dropping would still leave a passing import.\n- One-way import: `_processors_status` must not import `_processors`, asserted directly.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `_processors.py` and `_processors_status.py` are each at or below 650 lines; checked by the\n      module-size gate with both counts recorded.\n- [ ] `process_status` still resolves from `rebar.reducer._processors`; checked by importing it by\n      that path and running the three named test modules unedited.\n- [ ] Replaying a fixture ticket yields byte-identical compiled state before and after; checked by\n      comparing the serialized state against a pre-move capture.\n- [ ] `_processors_status.py` imports nothing from `_processors`; checked by an explicit assertion on\n      the module's imports.\n- [ ] The full existing test suite passes with no test-file edits; checked by the gating test command.","id":"ce02-47ed-6505-4925","parent_id":"0e68-41eb-5782-4336","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Size relief: extract the STATUS cluster from reducer/_processors.py into _processors_status.py"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786162200231243001,"uuid":"b2be2bc3-9cab-4d93-b9f1-6162b67bc451"}